From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: add the IPv4 flow offload 
 engine

Add the nftables flowtable offload engine for IPv4 NAT flows: the
.ndo_setup_tc block binding (and his indirect block for bridge and VLAN
devices, which binds tc blocks only, never a flowtable: the next patch
drops it), the parsing of the flowtable's match and actions (Ethernet,
IPv4 and port mangles, VLAN push and pop, redirect), and the programming
of host, nexthop, public IP and flow entries for a flow that translates
one end only (SNAT, or DNAT of packets addressed to this router's own
address on the device they arrive through). Egress is an EDMA port or a
VLAN on one; the DSA user-port egress, for which the nexthop's S-tag and
C-tag inserts emulate the rtl8_4 head tag, and with it the follower
below, is carried but declined until a later patch sets up the conduit's
egress TPIDs. Each direction of a connection is its own flow and is
accepted or declined on its own; what its checks find outside this scope
is declined and stays in software. It does not yet compare the rule's
dissector keys with those it reads: the next patch adds that as it wires
the engine in, before anything can reach it.

It also carries the per-flow statistics for the flowtable, the TCP
control-flag and IPv4 fragment exceptions, the re-arm of a flow whose
entry stopped matching, the follower that re-points a port-addressed flow
when the bridge learns its client on another port, the table-full
reporting, and the per-port L3 interface programming in edma_port.c.

The engine is inert here: no block is bound, NETIF_F_HW_TC is not set,
and nothing calls into it. The next patch wires it in, off by default.

Kept as in his tree, and unreachable here: the re-arm of a flow whose
counter did not move and the follower's flap drop release a flow's host,
nexthop and public IP entries without deleting its flow entry first;
destroy, teardown and replace's unwind after a failed allocation or
insert release them even when the delete fails; and replace's unwind and
the follower's re-point release them after an add that failed but may
have landed (its counter clear and the delete taking it back out both
failed, or its result never came). The next patch replaces these paths:
a flow's entries are let go of only once its flow entry is confirmed
deleted, and an add that may have landed reports so; the flow then stays
tracked for disable to retry its delete or, when a hash-mode add never
returned its index, is counted as lost and keeps what it points at for
good, so that disable writes nothing back.

Ported from Kamil Bienkiewicz's flow offload series for the GL-BE9300
(perceival/openwrt-flint3 2365932733), his patches 0419, 0420, 0422,
0424, 0427, 0428, 0435, 0436, 0439, 0440, 0442, 0444, 0446, 0448-0450,
0452, 0453, 0455 and 0456, the IPv4 parts of 0451, and the engine parts
of 0434 (the PMTU taken from the redirect target) and 0441 (the NAT
encoder fields), folded into their final state. Changes in the port:
IPv6 routing, the tag_8021q encapsulation and his rtl8_4 parser alias
(0445, with the conduit's S-VLAN egress type of 0449) are left out, so
every flow towards a DSA user port is declined; the wiring at probe
(block binding, NETIF_F_HW_TC, the L3 interface programming at port
bring-up) is left to the next patch; his 0423 (NETIF_F_HW_TC) comes with
it; Kconfig keeps his NET_DSA (0445), BRIDGE and VLAN_8021Q (0449)
dependencies, leaves out IPV6 (0451) and adds INET, which the engine
calls into; every flow is declined unless the IPv4 fragment exception
reads back (his tree gated only IPv6 on its header exceptions);
ppe_offload_flow_forget() is moved out of the DSA-only block (his 0452
calls it on the re-arm path), the exception registers of his register
map come with this patch, which programs them, and comments that pointed
at code not carried are reworded. His 0440 and 0441 carry no
Signed-off-by in his tree; this port carries none.

