--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -0,0 +1,2658 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * Copyright (c) Qualcomm Technologies, Inc. and/or its subsidiaries.
+ */
+
+/* PPE hardware flow-offload TC hook -- .ndo_setup_tc entry point for
+ * this driver's netdevices, modeled on drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/
+ * mtk_ppe_offload.c.
+ *
+ * Scope of what this accepts, deliberately narrow for a first working
+ * cut and enforced by outright declining (-EOPNOTSUPP) anything
+ * outside it, falling back to the kernel's normal software forwarding
+ * path:
+ *
+ * - IPv4 with NAT only; IPv6 is declined (not ported here).
+ * - TCP, UDP or UDP-Lite only. IPv4 fragments are punted from a flow hit
+ *   by a parser exception programmed at init, see
+ *   ppe_offload_hdr_exception_init(); without it IPv4 is declined.
+ * - IPv4: single-ended NAT only: either the source address (and optionally
+ *   the source port) is rewritten and the destination is not -- SNAT,
+ *   the LAN->WAN direction of a masqueraded connection and the reply
+ *   direction of a port forward -- or the destination is rewritten and
+ *   the source is not -- DNAT, the WAN->LAN reply of a masquerade and
+ *   the inbound direction of a port forward. Each direction of a
+ *   connection is its own flow_cls_offload with its own cookie and is
+ *   accepted or declined on its own. A flow that rewrites both ends
+ *   (hairpin NAT) or neither (plain routing) is declined: the two NAT
+ *   overlays of the flow entry are the only ones implemented. A DNAT
+ *   flow is accepted only when the address it translates is this
+ *   router's own on the interface (or VLAN upper) the packets arrive
+ *   through: the flow entry has no field for a PPPoE session (the
+ *   rule's FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE, generic patch 714), so a PPPoE
+ *   WAN's reply would otherwise be programmed as a plain-IPv4 entry
+ *   that a spoofed frame on the WAN segment could hit while software
+ *   drops it.
+ *   The same check keeps a DNAT flow entering through a DSA user port
+ *   (a redirect from the LAN) in software, and likewise one entering a
+ *   WAN that is a bridge over the EDMA port: the address is the
+ *   bridge's, the rule's ingress is the port, and that is deliberate
+ *   for now.
+ * - Egress device must be one of this driver's own EDMA port
+ *   netdevices (edma_port_is_edma_netdev()), or a DSA user port whose
+ *   conduit is one. A tagged WAN (e.g. wan.10) arrives as that plain
+ *   EDMA device plus a FLOW_ACTION_VLAN_PUSH: the
+ *   flowtable resolves the VLAN upper through dev_fill_forward_path()
+ *   and hands the tag over as the push, which the nexthop's C-VLAN
+ *   tag-insert fields carry (a redirect to the VLAN netdevice itself is
+ *   resolved down via vlan_dev_real_dev() for the same programming).
+ *   The push must match a VLAN upper really stacked on that device,
+ *   whose MTU then bounds the flow's PMTU; a flow back out of its own
+ *   ingress port, a DSA conduit as egress, and a push with a PCP are
+ *   declined. A second/S-tag push, or any other VLAN proto than 802.1Q, is
+ *   declined -- untested, and this hardware's nexthop entry only has
+ *   a single C-VLAN + single S-VLAN insert field to begin with.
+ * - A DSA user port as egress (the LAN side of this board, mirroring
+ *   mtk_flow_get_dsa_port()) is programmed as the conduit's PPE port,
+ *   and the switch is told which port the frame is for through the
+ *   nexthop's tag inserts, since hardware cannot run the tagger:
+ *   see ppe_offload_dsa_egress(). The port is taken from the DSA
+ *   structures of the redirect target only; a port of a VLAN-aware
+ *   bridge, a port behind a LAG conduit, a VLAN push on top of a DSA
+ *   port, a tagger other than rtl8_4, and a conduit whose egress
+ *   TPIDs are not set up for the emulated head tag are all declined.
+ *   Where the inserts name the port outright (rtl8_4), a client that
+ *   its own VLAN-unaware bridge later learns on another of its ports
+ *   has its flows re-pointed there, see ppe_offload_fdb_event().
+ * - Tagged ingress (the reply direction on a tagged WAN) arrives as a
+ *   FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_VLAN match on the real device plus a
+ *   FLOW_ACTION_VLAN_POP. The flow key cannot carry the VLAN, only the
+ *   port's ingress L3 interface, so this is accepted with the collapse
+ *   described under SECURITY LIMITATION below, and only when the VID
+ *   names a VLAN upper really stacked on that device. Tagged ingress on
+ *   a DSA user port (a VLAN-aware bridge) is declined.
+ * - The flow must carry a FLOW_ACTION_MANGLE for the Ethernet header:
+ *   this is the *only* source this code uses for the nexthop's
+ *   destination MAC, deliberately -- the kernel has already resolved
+ *   the correct next-hop MAC via its own routing/neighbor tables by
+ *   the time it offers the flow, so there's no reason to duplicate
+ *   that resolution here (and every reason not to, since getting it
+ *   wrong would misdirect real traffic).
+ * - FLOW_CLS_STATS is implemented via the separate per-flow counter
+ *   table (the flow entry's own "age" field is not a packet/byte
+ *   counter). Counters are cumulative and not clear-on-read, so each
+ *   tracked flow keeps its previous reading and reports a delta, and
+ *   refreshes lastused only when the packet count actually moves --
+ *   which is what stops the flowtable ageing out a connection that
+ *   hardware is still forwarding.
+ *
+ * - TCP is accepted only because the parser's control-flag exceptions
+ *   (FIN/SYN/RST -> redirect to CPU + de-accelerate, armed on an L3
+ *   flow hit) are programmed and read back at init; see
+ *   ppe_offload_tcp_exception_init(). Without them the flowtable's
+ *   FIN|RST mask cannot be honoured and a teardown would be
+ *   accelerated, leaving conntrack holding closed connections at
+ *   timeouts[ESTABLISHED] instead of timeouts[CLOSE].
+ *
+ *   With both directions of a connection in hardware these exceptions
+ *   are the ONLY thing that returns a FIN or RST to software, in either
+ *   direction: nothing else sets NF_FLOW_CLOSING. Do not weaken them.
+ *
+ * - SECURITY LIMITATION: a flow entry is keyed on the 5-tuple plus an
+ *   ingress L3 interface, and nothing finer. ppe_offload_ingress_edma_dev()
+ *   resolves a VLAN upper and a DSA user port down to the conduit, so
+ *   every DSA port and every VLAN on that conduit collapse into one
+ *   src_l3_if -- whereas the software flowtable key it stands in for
+ *   carries the exact iifidx. Where two firewall zones share a conduit
+ *   (this board runs a trusted and a guest VLAN over the same one), a
+ *   host in the weaker zone can source-spoof a victim's offloaded
+ *   5-tuple and have silicon forward and translate its packets with no
+ *   software inspection -- no rp_filter, no interface-scoped rule. The
+ *   same holds for the VLANs of the WAN port once tagged ingress is
+ *   accepted: a flow entered through wan.10 also matches the 5-tuple
+ *   arriving untagged on wan, and vice versa. The
+ *   hardware offers no finer qualifier, so this is a property of
+ *   enabling the flowtable across such a boundary, not something this
+ *   driver can validate away.
+ *
+ * Flows entering on the WAN port hit in hardware (measured by Kamil
+ * Bienkiewicz). Flows entering on an rtl8_4 conduit do not: with the
+ * reset TPIDs the parser stops at the 0x8899 head tag, so their entries
+ * never hit.
+ *
+ * Ported from Kamil Bienkiewicz's flow-offload series for the GL-BE9300
+ * (perceival/openwrt-flint3); IPv6 routing, the tag_8021q encapsulation
+ * and the ingress parser alias for rtl8_4 are not carried here.
+ */
+
